{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EHgYXu9QcaSGwfEC1CvY99","type":"tool_result","content":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet benchmarks may be substantially redundant, so a few runs might carry information about the rest. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a family of selector--imputer pairs built from a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers; the identity of the exact source tree evaluated in the sealed run is not established by the retained artifacts, and we presume it is the development-selected pair or its latent-skill extension. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The evaluated pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{Hendrycks2021MATH}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQA}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSR} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro}; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n111:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n122:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n154:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n164:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n203:\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n235:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n241:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n292:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n[('ref.openalex.W3083410900', 'Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding'), ('ref.openalex.W4386576685', 'MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark'), ('ref.openalex.W4320003957', 'Holistic Evaluation of Language Models'), ('ref.openalex.W7133239970', 'Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena'), ('ref.openalex.W4392181972', 'tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples'), ('ref.openalex.W4406058560', 'metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models'), ('ref.openalex.W4403662613', 'Do These LLM Benchmarks Agree? Fixing Benchmark Evaluation with BenchBench'), ('ref.openalex.W4405254278', 'Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families'), ('ref.openalex.W4400023054', 'BigCodeBench: Benchmarking Code Generation with Diverse Function Calls and Complex Instructions'), ('ref.openalex.W2049633694', 'Maximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm'), ('ref.openalex.W28412257', 'Stacked generalization'), ('ref.openalex.W2914369697', 'Stacked Regressions'), ('ref.openalex.W2611328865', 'Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization'), ('ref.openalex.W2137245235', 'Probabilistic Matrix Factorization'), ('ref.openalex.W2131824593', 'Near-Optimal Sensor Placements in Gaussian Processes: Theory, Efficient Algorithms and Empirical Studies'), ('ref.openalex.W1680189815', 'An analysis of approximations for maximizing submodular set functions—I'), ('ref.openalex.W2100358124', 'Inference and Missing Data'), ('ref.openalex.W2014018052', 'Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review'), ('ref.openalex.W1648888400', 'Item response theory for psychologists'), ('ref.openalex.W4411630215', 'Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples'), ('ref.openalex.W4415798639', 'Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance'), ('ref.openalex.W4234698323', 'Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems'), ('ref.openalex.W2075665712', 'Solving Least Squares Problems'), ('ref.openalex.W1517928915', 'Near-optimal observation selection using submodular functions'), ('ref.openalex.W4401042935', 'Efficient Benchmarking (of Language Models)'), ('ref.openalex.W4281690148', 'Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models'), ('ref.openalex.W3171135604', 'Comparing Test Sets with Item Response Theory')]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d1aba0e-ef79-4f71-9ae1-c5a455e170f8","uuid":"3a8861fc-afad-4222-a25e-2d05b0d12232","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:21:09.265Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet benchmarks may be substantially redundant, so a few runs might carry information about the rest. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a family of selector--imputer pairs built from a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers; the identity of the exact source tree evaluated in the sealed run is not established by the retained artifacts, and we presume it is the development-selected pair or its latent-skill extension. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The evaluated pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{Hendrycks2021MATH}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQA}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSR} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro}; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n111:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n122:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n154:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n164:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n203:\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n235:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n241:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n292:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n[('ref.openalex.W3083410900', 'Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding'), ('ref.openalex.W4386576685', 'MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark'), ('ref.openalex.W4320003957', 'Holistic Evaluation of Language Models'), ('ref.openalex.W7133239970', 'Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena'), ('ref.openalex.W4392181972', 'tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples'), ('ref.openalex.W4406058560', 'metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models'), ('ref.openalex.W4403662613', 'Do These LLM Benchmarks Agree? Fixing Benchmark Evaluation with BenchBench'), ('ref.openalex.W4405254278', 'Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families'), ('ref.openalex.W4400023054', 'BigCodeBench: Benchmarking Code Generation with Diverse Function Calls and Complex Instructions'), ('ref.openalex.W2049633694', 'Maximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm'), ('ref.openalex.W28412257', 'Stacked generalization'), ('ref.openalex.W2914369697', 'Stacked Regressions'), ('ref.openalex.W2611328865', 'Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization'), ('ref.openalex.W2137245235', 'Probabilistic Matrix Factorization'), ('ref.openalex.W2131824593', 'Near-Optimal Sensor Placements in Gaussian Processes: Theory, Efficient Algorithms and Empirical Studies'), ('ref.openalex.W1680189815', 'An analysis of approximations for maximizing submodular set functions—I'), ('ref.openalex.W2100358124', 'Inference and Missing Data'), ('ref.openalex.W2014018052', 'Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review'), ('ref.openalex.W1648888400', 'Item response theory for psychologists'), ('ref.openalex.W4411630215', 'Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples'), ('ref.openalex.W4415798639', 'Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance'), ('ref.openalex.W4234698323', 'Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems'), ('ref.openalex.W2075665712', 'Solving Least Squares Problems'), ('ref.openalex.W1517928915', 'Near-optimal observation selection using submodular functions'), ('ref.openalex.W4401042935', 'Efficient Benchmarking (of Language Models)'), ('ref.openalex.W4281690148', 'Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models'), ('ref.openalex.W3171135604', 'Comparing Test Sets with Item Response Theory')]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
